Let $f, g \in \mathbb{C}\{z\}$ be two holomorphic germs at the origin of the form
$$f(z) = \lambda z + a_{p+1} z^{p+1} + a_{p+2} z^{p+2} + \cdots, \qquad g(z) = \mu z + b_{q+1} z^{q+1} + b_{q+2} z^{q+2} + \cdots,$$
with $\lambda^n = 1$ and $\mu^m = 1$ for some positive integers $n, m$ (i.e., $f$ and $g$ are parabolic germs of one complex variable, with $0$ a fixed point whose multiplier is a root of unity). To each such parabolic germ one associates the following holomorphic conjugacy invariants:
\begin{itemize}
  \item the multiplier $f'(0)$;
  \item the parabolic multiplicity (the integer $\nu$ such that, after iterating to make the multiplier equal to $1$, the first nonvanishing higher-order term has degree $\nu+1$);
  \item the iterative residue (the formal residue at $0$ of $dz/(z - f^{\circ k}(z))$ for an appropriate iterate);
  \item the Écalle–Voronin functional invariants, i.e., the equivalence class of the tuple of horn maps obtained from the Fatou coordinates on the attracting and repelling petals, which together with the discrete data above form a complete system of invariants for local holomorphic conjugacy.
\end{itemize}
Two such germs $f$ and $g$ are said to be holomorphically locally conjugate if there exists a germ of biholomorphism $h \in \mathbb{C}\{z\}$ with $h(0) = 0$ and $h \circ f = g \circ h$ near $0$.

Question. Does there exist an effective decision procedure (an algorithm taking as input finite descriptions of $f$ and $g$, e.g. their convergent power-series coefficients given by computable approximations together with effective bounds) that, in finite time, decides whether $f$ and $g$ are holomorphically locally conjugate? In particular, can the comparison of the Écalle–Voronin functional invariants — which are equivalence classes of pairs of holomorphic horn maps and are generally regarded as intractable — be made algorithmically decidable?

